    "narrative": "The evidence did not separate the two explanations. The run ended inconclusive, so it could not say whether the weakest region’s on-time delivery reflects a real service-quality gap or is mainly explained by the order volume that region carries.\n\nThe claim that the weakest region is genuinely lower on on-time delivery even after accounting for order volume remained unresolved. The competing claim — that higher order volume explains the lower on-time delivery — was weakened, but not rejected, so it lost some support without being ruled out. In other words, neither explanation won cleanly.\n\nThe question was tested with two correlation analyses and two simple regressions. The loop stopped because there was insufficient evidence, and three questions remained open: whether the gap persists after controlling for order volume, whether the weakest region actually has unusually high volume, and whether stronger evidence would support the service-quality-gap explanation.",
    "statement": "Mixed evidence for: The weakest region carries higher order volume than other regions, and that higher volume explains its lower on-time delivery.",
